PRACTICAL: INSTALL GOOGLE APP ENGINE AND CREATE HELLO WORLD APPLICATION USING PYTHON

====================================================================
AIM
====================================================================

To install and configure Google App Engine and create a Hello World
web application using Python Flask.

====================================================================
REQUIREMENTS
====================================================================

1. Google Account
2. Internet Connection
3. Web Browser

Open:
https://console.cloud.google.com

====================================================================
STEP 1 : CREATE GOOGLE CLOUD PROJECT
====================================================================

1. Open Google Cloud Console
2. Click:
   Select Project → NEW PROJECT
3. Project Name:
   hello-app
4. Click CREATE

====================================================================
STEP 2 : OPEN CLOUD SHELL
====================================================================

1. Click terminal icon (>_) from top-right corner
2. Cloud Shell terminal opens

====================================================================
STEP 3 : CREATE APP ENGINE APPLICATION
====================================================================

Run command:

gcloud app create

Choose region:
6

Region Name:
asia-south1

====================================================================
STEP 4 : ENABLE REQUIRED APIS
====================================================================

Run:

gcloud services enable cloudbuild.googleapis.com

Run:

gcloud services enable appengine.googleapis.com

Wait 1–2 minutes.

====================================================================
STEP 5 : CREATE PROJECT FOLDER
====================================================================

Run:

mkdir hello-world
cd hello-world

====================================================================
STEP 6 : CREATE main.py
====================================================================

Run:

nano main.py

Paste following code:

from flask import Flask

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def hello():
    return "Hello World from Google App Engine!"

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(host='127.0.0.1', port=8080, debug=True)

Save File:
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X

====================================================================
STEP 7 : CREATE requirements.txt
====================================================================

Run:

nano requirements.txt

Paste:

Flask==3.0.0
gunicorn==21.2.0

Save:
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X

====================================================================
STEP 8 : CREATE app.yaml
====================================================================

Run:

nano app.yaml

Paste:

runtime: python311

entrypoint: gunicorn -b :$PORT main:app

Save:
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X

====================================================================
STEP 9 : CHECK FILES
====================================================================

Run:

ls

Output:

app.yaml
main.py
requirements.txt

====================================================================
STEP 10 : DEPLOY APPLICATION
====================================================================

Run:

gcloud app deploy

Type:

Y

Wait for deployment to complete.

====================================================================
STEP 11 : OPEN WEBSITE
====================================================================

Run:

gcloud app browse

OR manually open:

https://PROJECT-ID.appspot.com

Output:

Hello World from Google App Engine!

====================================================================
PROJECT STRUCTURE
====================================================================

hello-world/
│
├── main.py
├── requirements.txt
└── app.yaml

====================================================================
CODE EXPLANATION
====================================================================

1. from flask import Flask
   Imports Flask web framework.

2. app = Flask(__name__)
   Creates Flask application object.

3. @app.route('/')
   Defines homepage URL route.

4. def hello():
   Function executed when homepage opens.

5. return "Hello World from Google App Engine!"
   Displays message on webpage.

====================================================================
IMPORTANT FILES
====================================================================

1. main.py
   Contains application code.

2. requirements.txt
   Contains required Python libraries.

3. app.yaml
   App Engine configuration file.

====================================================================
IMPORTANT COMMANDS
====================================================================

gcloud app create
Creates App Engine application.

gcloud app deploy
Deploys application to cloud.

gcloud app browse
Opens deployed application.

nano filename
Opens file editor.

ls
Displays files.

====================================================================
VIVA QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS
====================================================================

Q1. What is Google App Engine?
Ans:
Google App Engine is a cloud platform used to develop and host web applications.

Q2. Which cloud model does App Engine use?
Ans:
PaaS (Platform as a Service)

Q3. What is Flask?
Ans:
Flask is a lightweight Python web framework.

Q4. What is app.yaml?
Ans:
Configuration file used by App Engine.

Q5. What is requirements.txt?
Ans:
File containing required Python libraries.

Q6. What is deployment?
Ans:
Uploading application to cloud server.

Q7. What is Cloud Shell?
Ans:
Browser-based Linux terminal provided by Google Cloud.

Q8. What is routing?
Ans:
Routing connects URLs with functions.

Example:
@app.route('/')

Q9. What is gunicorn?
Ans:
Gunicorn is a Python WSGI HTTP server.

Q10. What is scalability?
Ans:
Ability to handle increasing users automatically.

Q11. What is WSGI?
Ans:
Web Server Gateway Interface used between Python web apps and servers.

Q12. Difference between localhost and cloud deployment?

Localhost:
Runs on own computer.

Cloud Deployment:
Runs on Google cloud servers.

Q13. Why did deployment error occur earlier?
Ans:
Cloud Build API/service account was not enabled.

Q14. What is runtime in app.yaml?

Example:
runtime: python311

It specifies Python runtime environment.

====================================================================
CONCLUSION
====================================================================

Successfully configured Google App Engine, created Python Flask web
application, deployed application on cloud and accessed hosted website

